      "## Common Pitfalls & Mistakes to Avoid",
      "Be sure to avoid these frequent calculation mistakes:\n\n- Confusing HCF (largest factor) with LCM (smallest multiple).\n- Assuming the identity HCF × LCM = A × B holds for three or more numbers (it only holds for two numbers).\n- Forgetting to simplify prime factors completely down to prime bases.",
      "## Frequently Asked Questions",
      "### What is LCM and HCF?",
      "LCM (Least Common Multiple) is the smallest positive number that is a multiple of two or more numbers. HCF (Highest Common Factor), also called GCD (Greatest Common Divisor), is the largest number that divides two or more numbers cleanly. For example, for 12 and 18, HCF is 6 (largest number dividing both) and LCM is 36 (smallest number divisible by both).",
      "### What is the relationship formula between LCM and HCF?",
      "For any two positive integers A and B, the product of their HCF and LCM is strictly equal to the product of the numbers themselves: HCF(A, B) × LCM(A, B) = A × B. If you know any three values, you can instantly solve for the fourth: LCM(A, B) = (A × B) ÷ HCF(A, B).",
      "### How do you find LCM and HCF using prime factorization?",
      "First, write out the prime factorizations of all numbers. To find HCF, multiply the smallest power of each common prime factor. To find LCM, multiply the highest power of every prime factor present across all numbers.",
      "### Does HCF × LCM = A × B work for three numbers?",
